1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,480
Would your end-to-end tests catch a bug? I built e2e-mutate to find out.

2
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:19,200
e2e tests heal themselves. When the app changes, the agent finds another way to the goal. That keeps tests green through UI changes. But does it also keep them green through real bugs? Nothing measures that today.

3
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:28,020
Here's a small bookshop with seven e2e tests. Some use strict checks, some use vague AI checks. They're all green, and every agent step replays from cache.

4
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:43,140
e2e-mutate plants forty realistic bugs into the running app, one at a time: a dead button, a save that stores nothing, a wrong price. Then it reruns only the tests that touch each bug. Twenty-seven of forty were caught. Six were healed past.

5
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:48,280
Here's one of them. The Cart link in the nav is broken. Watch the test, at four times speed.

6
00:00:48,740 --> 00:00:54,340
Replay taps the link. Nothing happens. The agent taps it again, then opens the cart page directly.

7
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:59,980
And the test passes. Users can't reach their cart from the nav, but the suite is green.

8
00:01:00,740 --> 00:01:06,120
The report lists every bug that got through, with the screen the test saw, and a concrete check that would have caught it.

9
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:15,440
It found a gap in a strict check too. The order total was hidden, and toHaveText still passed, because a test-id query also matches hidden elements.

10
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:23,400
So I followed the report. Check that totals are visible. Check the order status. After saving, reload and check the value was stored.

11
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:25,940
Then I ran the same forty bugs again.

12
00:01:26,780 --> 00:01:35,500
Sixty-eight percent caught became ninety-two. Healed past went from six to one. And it's built only on e2e's public APIs, with no change to e2e itself.
